Two defects that compose into "a PR merges while CI is pending". PR #160 did
exactly that. They ship together because neither is safe alone: fixing only the
gates makes every pipeline over 300s refuse to merge, and fixing only the check
leaves the fail-open path live for every other verdict.

WI-ninar — poll_ci asked a question its backend can never answer yes to.
It decided "has any job started?" by testing whether any status context had gone
NON-pending. That was correct for Forgejo Actions, which posted one context PER
JOB, so contexts flipped as individual jobs finished. Woodpecker posts exactly
ONE AGGREGATE context that stays pending for the entire pipeline, so the answer
was "no" for the whole run and every pipeline outliving CI_STALE_PENDING_SECONDS
(300s default, against a ~360s suite) was declared a hung runner. It was not
detecting anything; it was structurally unsatisfiable.

Now it asks whether a context EXISTS AT ALL. Woodpecker posts it when it creates
the run, so presence proves dispatch and absence is the dispatch failure the
check was written to catch. any_job_started -> run_dispatched. A parse failure
keeps polling rather than fabricating a hung verdict, so an unreadable backend
surfaces as an honest timeout (2), not a fake dispatch failure (3). The
threshold is deliberately NOT raised — that was the by-hand workaround that
forced #160 through, and it moves the false verdict instead of removing it.

WI-ditav — four merge gates enumerated the codes that BLOCK.
The filing named one site; the audit found four. merge-pr and auto-pr's
POST-REBASE merge path fell through to the merge on exit 3 as shipped; auto-pr's
flush and main paths would fall through on any code added later. Exit 3 was
introduced AFTER those guards were written, by a different fix, so a guard that
was correct the day it was authored silently became permission. poll_ci's own
header comment listed only 0/1/2 and never mentioned 3, which is plausibly how
four call sites came to omit it; the comment is fixed and now says callers may
not depend on it being complete.

The fix is a chokepoint, not "also handle 3" — that reproduces the same shape
one code later. ci_verdict_permits_merge is the single home for "may we merge on
this verdict?": 0 permits, everything else refuses and preserves its code, and
an unrecognised verdict says so rather than refusing silently. The two sites
that were ALREADY safe were safe for exactly the reason L54 gives — they branch
on rc==0 — which is the argument for making that the only spelling. Per-site
recovery text stays at the call sites, because flush/main/post-rebase
legitimately advise different next steps (L27). Retry-loop conditions are
untouched: "should we retry?" is a different question from "may we merge?".

Behavioural tests, no forge required: poll_ci is driven with api_get overridden
in the sourced shell, so the WI-ninar change is distinguished from the bug it
replaces on identical input — one pending aggregate context past the threshold
must NOT yield 3, while an empty status list still must (without that second
assertion the fix is indistinguishable from deleting the check). Mutation-tested
three ways; each turns exactly the corresponding test red.

Two notes on the tests themselves. The first recurrence guard was too strict and
false-positived on auto-pr's exit-2 RECOVERY branch (which compares against 2,
then re-polls); rather than add an exemption it is scoped to merge-pr, where the
absence of retry/recovery logic means any such comparison IS the merge gate, and
the reasoning is recorded in place. And test_autopr_exit2_retry asserted the
literal string "exit 2", which no longer appears now that escalations propagate
exit "$poll_result"; that assertion now checks propagation and defers the
stronger behavioural claim to the new suite.

Per-PR coverage is CLOSED, not merely disclosed: the new test's subject is
scripts/lib/*, which the top-level mapper deliberately does not map, so it is
named in the forge-arms step of .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml, which runs on any
scripts/** change.

Governance: scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh, scripts/merge-pr and scripts/auto-pr are
governance files. Design was filed on WI-ninar and approved by the human, along
with merging via auto-pr without --gov.

Branch protection is now an actual backstop too: the required check was
configured correctly on dev and main, but "Do not allow bypassing the above
settings" was unticked, so the rules did not apply to the merging account —
which is the only way a merge succeeds against a pending required check. The
human has ticked it on both.

Refs WI-ninar, WI-ditav

